<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/maui-valley.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/maui-valley-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/maui-valley.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="726" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.1"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><a href="/island-communities/">Island communities</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Kahului &amp; Wailuku</span></nav><p class="tag">Maui · Central Maui</p><h1>Insurance Appraiser for Kahului and Wailuku Claims</h1><p class="lede">Central Maui’s strong winds, dry isthmus and aging post-war homes shape the claims we see. We help Kahului and Wailuku owners resolve disputes over the cost of repairs.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn sun" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn line-light" href="/contact-hawaii-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="facts"><div><dt>County</dt><dd>Maui County</dd></div><div><dt>Island</dt><dd>Maui</dd></div><div><dt>Service</dt><dd>On-site inspections</dd></div></dl></div></section><div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><div class="local"><div><b class="h">Local claim risks</b><ul class="chips"><li>High trade winds</li><li>Wildfire and smoke</li><li>Kona storm rain and flooding</li><li>Plumbing and water losses</li></ul></div><div><b class="h">Neighborhoods we cover</b><ul class="chips soft"><li>Kahului</li><li>Wailuku</li><li>Waikapu</li><li>Waiehu</li><li>Wailuku Heights</li><li>Maui Lani</li><li>Puunene</li></ul></div><p><b>Housing:</b> Kahului has many post-war single-family homes built in planned neighborhoods from the 1950s on, Wailuku has older homes and historic buildings near Iao Valley, and newer subdivisions such as Maui Lani and Waikapu fill in between.</p><p><b>History:</b> Strong winds funnel through Maui’s central isthmus, the August 2023 fires included a blaze in Pulehu above central Maui, and Kona lows and heavy rain can flood streams near Wailuku.</p></div>
<h2>Central Maui’s housing</h2>
<p>Kahului and Wailuku form Maui’s population and business center on the isthmus between the West Maui Mountains and Haleakala. Much of Kahului was built after the war as planned neighborhoods of modest single-family homes, many now expanded, re-roofed and remodeled several times. Wailuku, the county seat, is older, with historic homes and commercial buildings near Market Street and neighborhoods climbing toward Iao Valley and Wailuku Heights. Waiehu has a mix of older and newer homes, and the Maui Lani and Waikapu areas add newer subdivisions and townhomes. Puunene, home to the former sugar mill, reflects the area’s plantation history.</p>
<p>Since 2023, Central Maui has also absorbed many households displaced from West Maui, adding pressure on contractors, rental housing and claim timelines.</p>
<h2>Wind, fire and rain</h2>
<p>Trade winds accelerate through the isthmus, so Kahului is often among the windiest places on the island. Strong gusts lift roofing, damage solar panels and topple trees. The dry central plain is also fire-prone; during the August 2023 Maui wildfires, a fire burned in the Pulehu area above central Maui, and smoke affected homes well beyond the fire lines. Kona lows and heavy rain can send water rushing down from the West Maui Mountains into streams around Wailuku.</p>
<h2>Common Central Maui disputes</h2>
<ul>
<li>Wind damage to roofs and solar arrays attributed to age, UV or poor installation.</li>
<li>Smoke and ash cleaning scopes that skip HVAC systems, attics or soft goods.</li>
<li>Additional living expense limits strained by the tight post-2023 rental market.</li>
<li>Estimates using pre-2023 pricing that no longer matches Maui labor and material costs.</li>
<li>Water damage from roof leaks during Kona storms labeled as long-term seepage.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Current Maui pricing</b>Contractor demand on Maui has changed labor and material costs. An estimate should reflect what it costs to repair your home now, including freight and General Excise Tax, not outdated price lists.</div>
<h2>How appraisal helps in Kahului and Wailuku</h2>
<p>If your insurer accepts the claim but its payment does not match what a Maui contractor would charge, the appraisal clause may resolve the amount without litigation. Appraisal does not decide coverage, and no outcome is guaranteed. For denials, claim-handling concerns or suit deadlines, consult a Hawaii attorney.</p>
<p>Hawaii Insurance Appraiser scopes and prices Central Maui losses with current local costs. Our <a href="/appraisal-specialties/kona-storm-high-wind-claims/">high-wind claims</a> service covers roof and solar damage, our <a href="/appraisal-specialties/wildfire-claim-appraisal/">wildfire claim appraisal</a> service covers smoke and fire losses, and the <a href="/island-claim-tools/hurricane-deductible-calculator/">hurricane deductible calculator</a> helps you understand which deductible may apply.</p>
<p>Keep a simple file for your claim: the policy, every estimate and letter from the insurer, photos taken right after the loss, and receipts for temporary repairs or living costs. A well-organized file helps any appraiser build an accurate estimate and makes it easier to see exactly where the carrier’s number falls short.</p>
<h2>Specialties available in Kahului &amp; Wailuku</h2><p>Every Hawaii Insurance Appraiser specialty is available to owners and associations in Kahului &amp; Wailuku, including:</p><ul class="cols2"><li><a href="/appraisal-specialties/appraisal-clause-representation/">Appraisal Clause Representation</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-specialties/homeowner-claim-appraisal/">Homeowner Claim Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-specialties/condo-aoao-commercial-appraisal/">Condo, AOAO &amp; Commercial</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-specialties/roof-hail-claim-appraisal/">Roof &amp; Hail Claims</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-specialties/kona-storm-high-wind-claims/">Kona Storm &amp; High-Wind Claims</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-specialties/hurricane-claim-appraisal/">Hurricane Claim Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-specialties/water-damage-claim-appraisal/">Water Damage Claims</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-specialties/fire-smoke-claim-appraisal/">Fire &amp; Smoke Claims</a></li></ul>
<h2>Questions from Kahului &amp; Wailuku owners</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Wind is constant in Kahului. Will my insurer still pay for wind damage?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>Normal trade winds are part of life, but sudden damage from a strong wind event is generally covered under windstorm coverage. Documentation of the date and the damage helps distinguish it from wear.</p></div></details><details><summary>My Wailuku home had smoke damage in 2023 but no fire. Is it too late to dispute the payment?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>It depends on your policy’s deadlines and Hawaii law. Review the policy’s suit and appraisal provisions promptly, and consult a Hawaii attorney if a deadline may be approaching.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does appraisal account for higher Maui construction costs after 2023?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>An appraisal estimate should reflect current local costs for labor and materials at the time of repair. Outdated pricing is a common reason estimates come in low.</p></div></details></div>
